## Why This Is Asked

This tests design for guardrails, data isolation, and cost controls in Azure OpenAI deployments, a common advanced requirement for regulated customers.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic safety policy engine
- Prompt redaction and blocking
- API Management routing
- Grounding with versioned KB (Blob + Cognitive Search)
- Per-tenant isolation (Cosmos DB) and quotas
- Telemetry and auditing (Data Lake)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal policy eval
function evaluatePrompt(prompt, policies) {
  for (const p of policies) {
    if (p.types?.some(t => prompt.includes(t))) {
      return {blocked: true, reason: p.reason}
    }
  }
  // simple redact example
  const redacted = prompt.replace(/SSN-\d{4}/g, '[REDACTED_SSN]')
  return {blocked: false, redacted}
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement per-tenant billing and quotas at API level?
- How would you test guardrails under burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes architectural decisions for multi-tenant Azure AI deployments with strict data residency, governance, and explainability requirements. It tests ability to plan region-aware routing, tenant-scoped knowledge bases, identity boundaries, and end-to-end auditing.

## Key Concepts

- Data residency and customer-managed keys (CMK) for storage
- Region-local Cognitive Search indexes per tenant
- Region-aware model routing for Azure OpenAI
- Per-tenant RBAC using Azure AD
- Explainability logging to Data Lake with traceability
- End-to-end testing including latency, privacy, and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo region routing for tenants
function getTenantRegion(tenantId) {
  const regionMap = {
    't1': 'eastus',
    't2': 'northeurope'
  };
  return regionMap[tenantId] || 'eastus';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate cross-region consistency and prove data residency?
- What failure modes require circuit breakers and fallback paths, and how would you implement them?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests CDS governance, real-world guardrails, and Azure AI integration.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant prompts
- PII redaction and safety checks
- Governance metadata and provenance
- Audit logging to Data Lake
- Fallback policies and test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// example wrapper pseudocode for gating
async function respond(input, tenantId){
  const redacted = redactPII(input);
  const ctx = await fetchContext(tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(ctx, redacted);
  const risk = await safetyCheck(prompt);
  if(!risk.ok) return fallbackResponse(ctx);
  const res = await openaiChat(prompt);
  const final = attachGovernance(res, tenantId, ctx.modelVer);
  await log(final);
  return final;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the end-to-end logging and searchability of prompts? 
- How would you handle model version rollouts with zero-downtime for tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests real-time, multi-tenant integration across Azure AI, telemetry, and automation. It emphasizes safety, RBAC, and auditable data flows under production load.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI integration with streaming signals
- Telemetry ingestion (Event Hubs/OpenTelemetry) and anomaly detection
- KB grounding with versioning in Cognitive Search
- Tenant-scoped remediation playbooks and guardrails
- Triggering Logic Apps runbooks and per-tenant RBAC
- PII redaction and Data Lake auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: Ingest telemetry and generate playbook
async function handleTelemetry(event) {
  const redacted = redactPII(event);
  const prompt = `Given telemetry: ${redacted}, provide a tenant-specific remediation...`;
  const res = await openai.generate(prompt);
  const playbook = parsePlaybook(res);
  await triggerLogicApps(playbook, event.tenantId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test per-tenant RBAC restrictions for the generated actions?
- How would you ensure the KB versioning doesn't drift during a live incident?
